(2248198, 305204, 25884, '<td valign="top">Hi from the critique club,<br/> <br/>well done in this challenge! You met the challenge and you did it in style<br/>(nice shoes and stickings by the way). When i first saw this image i thought, i see the shoe, but wheres the other element. Then i saw that you used a short depth of field to show the feather. This is the main thing that brought your score down. The other thing that i would have liked to see was lighting that was a little less harsh. I''m wondering if you diffused the light at all? This would make the blown out skin a little less blown out and create more of a snese of realism rather then abstract. <br/> <br/>As i said before the main reason why i think this image was not voted very high was that the feathers were out of focus. What i would do to fix this is maybe wrap the shoe with the feathers or have a feather sticking out of the shoe, something creative. <br/> <br/>Something else i would have done to this photo is diffused the light. This can be done by simply wrapping some parchement paper or wax paper around the lamp, causing a diffused glow. I see in your comments you said that you didnt do very much PP. Although sometimes this is bad i think that this photo could have benefit from a little bit more post processing. I would have cloned out the distracting white marks in the background and i would have run it through neat-image to soften the skin tomes a little bit.<br/> <br/>Overall i though this was a good technical photo. But it was lacking some crucial elements that would have mad eit score higher. <br/> <br/>Everything i said here is not meant to be offensive, its onl meant to help you.  If you have any comments or questions feel free to private message me and ill get back to you as soon as i can. <br/> <br/>Most of all keep taking photos, its the only way to get better!!<br/> <br/>-Dan Gruskin<br/>P.S. please ignore any grammer mistakes, as you are an english lit. student ;)</td>', 'Hi from the critique club, well done in this challenge! You met the challenge and you did it in style(nice shoes and stickings by the way). When i first saw this image i thought, i see the shoe, but wheres the other element. Then i saw that you used a short depth of field to show the feather. This is the main thing that brought your score down. The other thing that i would have liked to see was lighting that was a little less harsh. I''m wondering if you diffused the light at all? This would make the blown out skin a little less blown out and create more of a snese of realism rather then abstract.  As i said before the main reason why i think this image was not voted very high was that the feathers were out of focus. What i would do to fix this is maybe wrap the shoe with the feathers or have a feather sticking out of the shoe, something creative.  Something else i would have done to this photo is diffused the light. This can be done by simply wrapping some parchement paper or wax paper around the lamp, causing a diffused glow. I see in your comments you said that you didnt do very much PP. Although sometimes this is bad i think that this photo could have benefit from a little bit more post processing. I would have cloned out the distracting white marks in the background and i would have run it through neat-image to soften the skin tomes a little bit. Overall i though this was a good technical photo. But it was lacking some crucial elements that would have mad eit score higher.  Everything i said here is not meant to be offensive, its onl meant to help you.  If you have any comments or questions feel free to private message me and ill get back to you as soon as i can.  Most of all keep taking photos, its the only way to get better!! -Dan GruskinP.S. please ignore any grammer mistakes, as you are an english lit. student ;)', '2006-03-22 16:57:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(2248471, 305212, 48577, '<td valign="top">I''ve seen this before :-))<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=295467" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/449/thumb/295467.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/449/thumb/295467.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/449/thumb/295467.jpg"/></a> <br/> <br/>Of course, your''s is much more well done! Congratulations!</td>', 'I''ve seen this before :-))   Of course, your''s is much more well done! Congratulations!', '2006-03-22 17:45:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(2248574, 305431, 19978, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/>by strangeghost<br/> <br/><i>The first three parts of this critique are written based purely on examination of your photo. "Final thoughts" is written after reviewing your score, photographer''s comments, and voter comments.</i> <br/> <br/><b>TECHNIQUE</b> <br/>Nice focus and oof background (but not too oof). Nice control of the tones. The blacks are really black without a loss of detail. The white of the guitar face is awfully big and bland, but there probably wasn''t much detail there to begin with, so OK.  Tack-sharp with detail on the shoe stitching.  My only real technical beef is with the lighting, which looks pretty flat and may have been on-board flash? It''s hard to suggest how this might have been better lit though. See comments in composition.<br/> <br/><b>COMPOSITION</b> <br/>I fully realize what a tough challenge this was, with a limited selection of objects to choose from, but this composition leaves me feeling "so what?" A shoe with a feather stuck in it, another feather on a scrunchie (?) and a feather stuck in a guitar. There''s no logic or purpose or excitement presented, it''s all just kind of there. The unusual angle was an attempt to add some imbalance or drama, but didn''t really do it for me. I like the addition of the guitar, but again, it''s hard to see the logic that holds them together, other than your title, which leads me to the issue of humor. Is it funny? Well, it''s got potential, but you didn''t get a belly laugh out of me. My guess is that you were struggling for a response to the challenge and threw this together as a last minute entry. There were probably creative solutions that you could have found to the humor issue, but I can hardly speak to these since I (ahem) didn''t enter this challenge. You know what they say about people who live in glass houses not throwing stones...<br/> <br/><b>EMOTIONAL IMPACT</b> <br/>Nope. I don''t think so. Technically excellent but emotionally ho-hum.<br/> <br/><b>FINAL THOUGHTS</b> <br/>Your photographer''s comment answers my "why" question. I was sorta correct, I guess, about your rush to get an entry together. Your final score of 5.0 is probably a bit of a disappointment, but not bad given the relative indifference of the voters (only 4 comments). Like I said at the outset, this was a tough challenge to enter, and I personally didn''t even take a shot, so bravo to you for jumping in.<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club by strangeghost The first three parts of this critique are written based purely on examination of your photo. "Final thoughts" is written after reviewing your score, photographer''s comments, and voter comments.  TECHNIQUE Nice focus and oof background (but not too oof). Nice control of the tones. The blacks are really black without a loss of detail. The white of the guitar face is awfully big and bland, but there probably wasn''t much detail there to begin with, so OK.  Tack-sharp with detail on the shoe stitching.  My only real technical beef is with the lighting, which looks pretty flat and may have been on-board flash? It''s hard to suggest how this might have been better lit though. See comments in composition. COMPOSITION I fully realize what a tough challenge this was, with a limited selection of objects to choose from, but this composition leaves me feeling "so what?" A shoe with a feather stuck in it, another feather on a scrunchie (?) and a feather stuck in a guitar. There''s no logic or purpose or excitement presented, it''s all just kind of there. The unusual angle was an attempt to add some imbalance or drama, but didn''t really do it for me. I like the addition of the guitar, but again, it''s hard to see the logic that holds them together, other than your title, which leads me to the issue of humor. Is it funny? Well, it''s got potential, but you didn''t get a belly laugh out of me. My guess is that you were struggling for a response to the challenge and threw this together as a last minute entry. There were probably creative solutions that you could have found to the humor issue, but I can hardly speak to these since I (ahem) didn''t enter this challenge. You know what they say about people who live in glass houses not throwing stones... EMOTIONAL IMPACT Nope. I don''t think so. Technically excellent but emotionally ho-hum. FINAL THOUGHTS Your photographer''s comment answers my "why" question. I was sorta correct, I guess, about your rush to get an entry together. Your final score of 5.0 is probably a bit of a disappointment, but not bad given the relative indifference of the voters (only 4 comments). Like I said at the outset, this was a tough challenge to enter, and I personally didn''t even take a shot, so bravo to you for jumping in.', '2006-03-22 18:06:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(2248941, 305399, 12448, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>This is a wonderful studio shot.  Well lit, composition is well done, color is there.  So why didn''t it do better?  Good question!  I''m guessing simply because while it''s a beautiful technically well done shot, there is nothing that really stands out and yells, "LOOK AT ME!"  The color certainly grabs my attention but that is about all that holds my attention.  I agree with the one comment about cloning out the lettering inside the shoe, it is a bit distracting and pulls your attention away from the beautiful shoe itself.<br/> <br/>Also, maybe just a slightly different set up could really bring this shot out, take the back shoe, set it upright and put the front shoe inside so it''s taller and really stands out, arrange them so you have a V shape or something to that effect, the toe of the shoe on the left bringing you in, following the line all the way to the heel of the other other, to the top of the other shoe and then follow that same line out the other side.  Just a thought that crossed my mind.<br/> <br/>Again, great shot overall.  Good luck in future challenges!<br/> <br/>Deannda</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! This is a wonderful studio shot.  Well lit, composition is well done, color is there.  So why didn''t it do better?  Good question!  I''m guessing simply because while it''s a beautiful technically well done shot, there is nothing that really stands out and yells, "LOOK AT ME!"  The color certainly grabs my attention but that is about all that holds my attention.  I agree with the one comment about cloning out the lettering inside the shoe, it is a bit distracting and pulls your attention away from the beautiful shoe itself. Also, maybe just a slightly different set up could really bring this shot out, take the back shoe, set it upright and put the front shoe inside so it''s taller and really stands out, arrange them so you have a V shape or something to that effect, the toe of the shoe on the left bringing you in, following the line all the way to the heel of the other other, to the top of the other shoe and then follow that same line out the other side.  Just a thought that crossed my mind. Again, great shot overall.  Good luck in future challenges! Deannda', '2006-03-22 19:34:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(2249433, 305212, 5318, '<td valign="top">Nice.....YOUR THE PERFECT MODEL FOR THAT SHOT.. Congradulations!!!!!!!</td>', 'Nice.....YOUR THE PERFECT MODEL FOR THAT SHOT.. Congradulations!!!!!!!', '2006-03-22 21:54:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(2249859, 305339, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>What a beautiful image you have presented for the Pick Two Challenge.  <br/> <br/>Certainly you were well rewarded for your care and consideration of the technicals - as well as composition.<br/> <br/>Obviously, there is little I can to by way of ''critique'' except add to the comments that tell you of "a job well done."<br/> <br/>However, I will suggest, that for your benefit and the benefit of those who would like to learn from this image, that you do fill in the blanks with the camera settings you used.  Those of us in the Critique Club continue to encourage all users who ask for a Critique to do this little chore.<br/> <br/>Again,well done.<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  What a beautiful image you have presented for the Pick Two Challenge.   Certainly you were well rewarded for your care and consideration of the technicals - as well as composition. Obviously, there is little I can to by way of ''critique'' except add to the comments that tell you of "a job well done." However, I will suggest, that for your benefit and the benefit of those who would like to learn from this image, that you do fill in the blanks with the camera settings you used.  Those of us in the Critique Club continue to encourage all users who ask for a Critique to do this little chore. Again,well done. Alice', '2006-03-23 00:13:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(2249884, 305301, 9906, '<td valign="top">From the Critique Club:<br/> <br/>This is kind of a hard image for me to critique because of the challenge restrictions.  You had a very defined set if criteria for your shot.  I think you met the challenge well.<br/> <br/>What I like:  I love the angle that you chose for this shot.  Both the camera angle and the tilted perspective.  I''m not usually a fan of tilted cropping, but I think it worked quite well here!  Great DOF.  What needs to be in focus is.  What needs to be seen but not necesarily in focus is just that.  Great colors except for...<br/> <br/>What I don''t like:  I''m a little put off by the fact that your whites ain''t quite white and you don''t really have any black in the shot.  Some work with levels would probably help with this.  I''m also a tad bit (though I see the humor) put off by the subject.  Not a lot of people in general like ''poop'' shots for lack of a better term.  This one was done tastefully but that is still what it is.<br/> <br/>Why it didn''t do so well in voting?  I think it is probably the subject matter.  See What I don''t like above.<br/> <br/>Keep on smiling and shooting!<br/>Yours<br/>TC</td>', 'From the Critique Club: This is kind of a hard image for me to critique because of the challenge restrictions.  You had a very defined set if criteria for your shot.  I think you met the challenge well. What I like:  I love the angle that you chose for this shot.  Both the camera angle and the tilted perspective.  I''m not usually a fan of tilted cropping, but I think it worked quite well here!  Great DOF.  What needs to be in focus is.  What needs to be seen but not necesarily in focus is just that.  Great colors except for... What I don''t like:  I''m a little put off by the fact that your whites ain''t quite white and you don''t really have any black in the shot.  Some work with levels would probably help with this.  I''m also a tad bit (though I see the humor) put off by the subject.  Not a lot of people in general like ''poop'' shots for lack of a better term.  This one was done tastefully but that is still what it is. Why it didn''t do so well in voting?  I think it is probably the subject matter.  See What I don''t like above. Keep on smiling and shooting!YoursTC', '2006-03-23 00:23:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(2251031, 305407, 32321, '<td valign="top">Hi! Heres a comment from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>First Impression:<br/>Busy! Eyes dropped to the large water drop front and centre, up to the dark area near the top, around to the left, back to the front water drop. <br/> <br/>Composition:<br/>I feel its too busy, with too much out of focus. The black areas on the screen are distracting in the bifocals as well as in the water drops. I like the colours.<br/>My eyes kept following the glasses frame around, looking for what was in focus.<br/> <br/>Subject:<br/>Clever use of bifocals and feathers. Meets challenge. Speed voters may have had trouble!<br/> <br/>Technical:<br/>Find DOF too shallow. If you could have brought the frame and some water drops in focus, your picture would have really popped<br/>Thanks for your comments describing what you did pre and post. Gives me a much better idea what your intentions were.<br/> Sorry, I dont understand your reference to RainX<br/> <br/>Summary:<br/>Congratulations on your 76% placement, and getting a score above your average.<br/></td>', 'Hi! Heres a comment from the Critique Club. First Impression:Busy! Eyes dropped to the large water drop front and centre, up to the dark area near the top, around to the left, back to the front water drop.  Composition:I feel its too busy, with too much out of focus. The black areas on the screen are distracting in the bifocals as well as in the water drops. I like the colours.My eyes kept following the glasses frame around, looking for what was in focus. Subject:Clever use of bifocals and feathers. Meets challenge. Speed voters may have had trouble! Technical:Find DOF too shallow. If you could have brought the frame and some water drops in focus, your picture would have really poppedThanks for your comments describing what you did pre and post. Gives me a much better idea what your intentions were. Sorry, I dont understand your reference to RainX Summary:Congratulations on your 76% placement, and getting a score above your average.', '2006-03-23 12:10:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(2251132, 305407, 8891, '<td valign="top">I might give this another try, perhaps, the set up is not awful to deal with. But I hope you all do understand about this focus thing...it''s a bit tough when your dealing with the camera lense, the close up filter and then two different areas of the lense prescription....I think...course if the prescription was the same for both areas...they probably would not be bi-focals.....hmmmm.</td>', 'I might give this another try, perhaps, the set up is not awful to deal with. But I hope you all do understand about this focus thing...it''s a bit tough when your dealing with the camera lense, the close up filter and then two different areas of the lense prescription....I think...course if the prescription was the same for both areas...they probably would not be bi-focals.....hmmmm.', '2006-03-23 12:40:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(2252245, 305081, 42792, '<td valign="top">Congratulations for successfully combining 2 unlikely subjects to look so artistic together.  Love how the green pops out.</td>', 'Congratulations for successfully combining 2 unlikely subjects to look so artistic together.  Love how the green pops out.', '2006-03-23 17:35:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(2252920, 305298, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>You had a great idea - as you know, another version of this idea is on the front page, right now.  And you scored well on your version as well.<br/> <br/>I have two thoughts on why you didn''t score even higher than you did (5.39 isn''t bad, you know).  The white feather boa is a little ''hot'' against the white background, and doesn''t really stand out very well.  A different colored backdrop would have been terrific, but we work with what we have.  In such cases, I usually recomment  bracketing your shots to get the most out of exposure and depth of field to try to get rid of the hot spots.<br/> <br/>The other problem, and a slight one, is the fact that those nice legs had to be held up by hands which showed.  If the legs could have been braced diferently, that probably would have made a big difference.<br/> <br/>Okay, all that said, you have a splendid image.  You were correct to tell your story simply.  Too much detail would have destroyed the poetry you have going here.<br/> <br/>Keep up the good work.  You''ll be our youngest front page winner yet.<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  You had a great idea - as you know, another version of this idea is on the front page, right now.  And you scored well on your version as well. I have two thoughts on why you didn''t score even higher than you did (5.39 isn''t bad, you know).  The white feather boa is a little ''hot'' against the white background, and doesn''t really stand out very well.  A different colored backdrop would have been terrific, but we work with what we have.  In such cases, I usually recomment  bracketing your shots to get the most out of exposure and depth of field to try to get rid of the hot spots. The other problem, and a slight one, is the fact that those nice legs had to be held up by hands which showed.  If the legs could have been braced diferently, that probably would have made a big difference. Okay, all that said, you have a splendid image.  You were correct to tell your story simply.  Too much detail would have destroyed the poetry you have going here. Keep up the good work.  You''ll be our youngest front page winner yet. Alice', '2006-03-23 21:59:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253028, 305083, 42567, '<td valign="top">Very nice picture!  Congrats on your top 10!!</td>', 'Very nice picture!  Congrats on your top 10!!', '2006-03-23 22:45:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253184, 305182, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club* <br/> <br/>"Too Drunk to Drive"  first off meet the challenge requirements, which is always a good place to start.  After reading your own comments I understand a few things a little better.  <br/> <br/>I did not realize the glass were used to spread the peanut butter.  I did understand the the chaos.  I wish you had been able to include a beer can or a glass that is specifically designed for alcohol (like martini).  Maybe darken the image a little would give it a grungier feel as well as maybe tipping over the PB.  I like the keys in the spilled liquid.  I think the flash might have been a bit much with the glow it has created on the plate.  The concept is a pretty hard one with such a crazy challenge subject to start with and I think was more then likely lost on most voters.</td>', '*Critique Club*  "Too Drunk to Drive"  first off meet the challenge requirements, which is always a good place to start.  After reading your own comments I understand a few things a little better.   I did not realize the glass were used to spread the peanut butter.  I did understand the the chaos.  I wish you had been able to include a beer can or a glass that is specifically designed for alcohol (like martini).  Maybe darken the image a little would give it a grungier feel as well as maybe tipping over the PB.  I like the keys in the spilled liquid.  I think the flash might have been a bit much with the glow it has created on the plate.  The concept is a pretty hard one with such a crazy challenge subject to start with and I think was more then likely lost on most voters.', '2006-03-24 00:01:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253246, 305144, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club* <br/> <br/>"A bicycle with peanut butter"  obviously by the titlle meets the crazy requirements of the Pick Two challenge.  <br/> <br/>I think based on the challenge it was hard to come up with a pretty picutre, by I do like the artistic feel of the image.  I really like how smooth and balanced the peanut butter is, you did not just throw it on.  I really like the way you shot this a little darker and edgier possibly to give more of that artistic feel.  The glow from the piece  that hold the petal (sorry for the lack of terminology) shows a nice focus and allows it too feel more balanced.  The levels in the piece flow very naturally nothing is harsh or forced.  <br/> <br/>Excellent close up.  Not too bad of a placement, but personally I thought it would finish higher I gave it a 7.  Keep up the  good work.</td>', '*Critique Club*  "A bicycle with peanut butter"  obviously by the titlle meets the crazy requirements of the Pick Two challenge.   I think based on the challenge it was hard to come up with a pretty picutre, by I do like the artistic feel of the image.  I really like how smooth and balanced the peanut butter is, you did not just throw it on.  I really like the way you shot this a little darker and edgier possibly to give more of that artistic feel.  The glow from the piece  that hold the petal (sorry for the lack of terminology) shows a nice focus and allows it too feel more balanced.  The levels in the piece flow very naturally nothing is harsh or forced.   Excellent close up.  Not too bad of a placement, but personally I thought it would finish higher I gave it a 7.  Keep up the  good work.', '2006-03-24 00:13:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253293, 305242, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club* <br/> <br/>"You know, it''s one of those days..."  does meet the criteria of the challenge... we have heels and we have peanut butter.  Hopefully your not too bummed about the score or placement, a lot of us (me included) have been there.  I personally think I have gotten better since then, but I think there is a learning curve.  Sorry for rambling, on to the photo.<br/> <br/>The subject itself is an interesting idea.  Maybe you had some trouble shrinking the photo down to meet the qualifications.  Possibly this is were it picked up all the noise that is coming across in the shot.  Then again maybe that is the look you wanted.  I am not sure if it is the lighting that brings out a hot spot on the feel or the noise, but it does take away from the score for me.  It was a nice try with such a crazy subject.  I look forward to seeing what you come up with next time!</td>', '*Critique Club*  "You know, it''s one of those days..."  does meet the criteria of the challenge... we have heels and we have peanut butter.  Hopefully your not too bummed about the score or placement, a lot of us (me included) have been there.  I personally think I have gotten better since then, but I think there is a learning curve.  Sorry for rambling, on to the photo. The subject itself is an interesting idea.  Maybe you had some trouble shrinking the photo down to meet the qualifications.  Possibly this is were it picked up all the noise that is coming across in the shot.  Then again maybe that is the look you wanted.  I am not sure if it is the lighting that brings out a hot spot on the feel or the noise, but it does take away from the score for me.  It was a nice try with such a crazy subject.  I look forward to seeing what you come up with next time!', '2006-03-24 00:24:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253351, 305433, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club* <br/> <br/>"Jell-O Martini with Peanut Butter Olive" is a nice addition into the Pick Two challenge.  I remeber flipping through the challenge and looking at your shot and thinking what a perfectly shaped peanut butter olive.  The smoothness you were able to make it pretty awesome in itself.  <br/> <br/>The image itself is very attention grabbing, pretty much the colors demand your attention.  I don''t know if I would have scored it higher with a white background but I can see how many voters probably would have preferred the white background (just based on past challenges).  I kind of liked the muted feel, where nothing is too flash like a lot of martini shots are; it feels very appropriate for the subject.  Also I am glad you don''t have any flash spots on the glass.  I am surprised you did not center the shot or place it more off centered, it just feels right in between.  Overall the shot was interesting and the olive really demanded attention.  Good work!</td>', '*Critique Club*  "Jell-O Martini with Peanut Butter Olive" is a nice addition into the Pick Two challenge.  I remeber flipping through the challenge and looking at your shot and thinking what a perfectly shaped peanut butter olive.  The smoothness you were able to make it pretty awesome in itself.   The image itself is very attention grabbing, pretty much the colors demand your attention.  I don''t know if I would have scored it higher with a white background but I can see how many voters probably would have preferred the white background (just based on past challenges).  I kind of liked the muted feel, where nothing is too flash like a lot of martini shots are; it feels very appropriate for the subject.  Also I am glad you don''t have any flash spots on the glass.  I am surprised you did not center the shot or place it more off centered, it just feels right in between.  Overall the shot was interesting and the olive really demanded attention.  Good work!', '2006-03-24 00:39:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253455, 305056, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>I thought it was interesting - I assumedd as shown in my voting comment that it was peanut butter, and I thought you had to be brave or stupid - with that amount of peanut butter on you!<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/> <br/>This is fine, not what I would call attractive with the eyes forced wide open and retouched-  though your comments I am unsure why you felt the need for this. <br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>Pick two .. you got it ... but that people had to guess peanut butter may have been the issue with the scoring.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>I really feel (personally) that this is a total black and white overkill ... that is only my opinion - lighting is fine.<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>As said ... add some colour so that people can see what you are doing - but I sharn''t harp on :)<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>Good picture - met the challenge - keep on shooting!<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. First Impression - the most important one: I thought it was interesting - I assumedd as shown in my voting comment that it was peanut butter, and I thought you had to be brave or stupid - with that amount of peanut butter on you! Composition:  This is fine, not what I would call attractive with the eyes forced wide open and retouched-  though your comments I am unsure why you felt the need for this.  Subject: Pick two .. you got it ... but that people had to guess peanut butter may have been the issue with the scoring. Technical (Colour and light): I really feel (personally) that this is a total black and white overkill ... that is only my opinion - lighting is fine. To grow its vote?: As said ... add some colour so that people can see what you are doing - but I sharn''t harp on :) Summary: Good picture - met the challenge - keep on shooting! If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari', '2006-03-24 01:13:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253475, 305278, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.<br/> <br/>Interesting to do a critique on your image but it is difficult if you don''t give us any information in your photographers comments.  When we do a critique, we go past just the photographic result, that''s what voters comments do.  The critique looks at what you were trying to achieve, how you wanted it to look and what issues you had in getting the image captured and ready for voting.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>What a way to meet the challenge.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/> <br/>Good ... there is nothing I would think is actually wrong with this shot.  Really good use of DOF.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>Meets the challenge and does it extremely well.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>Natural works for me .. almost all the time  - and this one is no different.<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>No ideas here .. as you did top 5!<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>Great pic .. well done on your finish ... :)<br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. Interesting to do a critique on your image but it is difficult if you don''t give us any information in your photographers comments.  When we do a critique, we go past just the photographic result, that''s what voters comments do.  The critique looks at what you were trying to achieve, how you wanted it to look and what issues you had in getting the image captured and ready for voting. First Impression - the most important one: What a way to meet the challenge. Composition:  Good ... there is nothing I would think is actually wrong with this shot.  Really good use of DOF. Subject: Meets the challenge and does it extremely well. Technical (Colour and light): Natural works for me .. almost all the time  - and this one is no different. To grow its vote?: No ideas here .. as you did top 5! Summary: Great pic .. well done on your finish ... :)If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari', '2006-03-24 01:18:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253499, 305237, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>Wow ... and she was the model and the photographer ... ignore the score what a great job!<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/> <br/>I really like this ... another of your commentors mentioned croping out the foot - wouldn''t work - but slowing the foot down may have - this would have allowed a larger blur ... I can''t figure out how you balanced on that jar!  Being picky ( and I can be sometimes) ... I would suggest unsing the lines on the floor to straighten the picture - this would create less distraction for the viewer.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>Great meets the challenge - and does it with style!<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>Good - nice lighting - interesting use of desat so the colour is only the peanut butter.<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>As above I think .. I can''t think of anything else at the moment.<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>I love it . .you have great ankles :) .. keep it up.<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. First Impression - the most important one: Wow ... and she was the model and the photographer ... ignore the score what a great job! Composition:  I really like this ... another of your commentors mentioned croping out the foot - wouldn''t work - but slowing the foot down may have - this would have allowed a larger blur ... I can''t figure out how you balanced on that jar!  Being picky ( and I can be sometimes) ... I would suggest unsing the lines on the floor to straighten the picture - this would create less distraction for the viewer. Subject: Great meets the challenge - and does it with style! Technical (Colour and light): Good - nice lighting - interesting use of desat so the colour is only the peanut butter. To grow its vote?: As above I think .. I can''t think of anything else at the moment. Summary: I love it . .you have great ankles :) .. keep it up. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari', '2006-03-24 01:24:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253530, 305285, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>Sexy wtihout being obvious.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>I think this is where you could have got more points .. aiming to have a little more on the left of the picture to put the shoe on thirds - having a little more at the top as well.  I am not sure how much you have cropped but that may have helped - just to give a little more of a line for the eye.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>Meets the challenge with no issues about it.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>I really like this .. I am glad you stated that these are the real colours - as I was thinking it was black and white overkill.  With 24 hours going out and picking up something with more colour wasn''t a real option.<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>As above - the cropping - or framing of the picture I think counted to the negative - but shot the person who gave you a one ... I can''t see how!<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>Love it .. great shot .. .keep shooting.<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. First Impression - the most important one: Sexy wtihout being obvious. Composition: I think this is where you could have got more points .. aiming to have a little more on the left of the picture to put the shoe on thirds - having a little more at the top as well.  I am not sure how much you have cropped but that may have helped - just to give a little more of a line for the eye. Subject: Meets the challenge with no issues about it. Technical (Colour and light): I really like this .. I am glad you stated that these are the real colours - as I was thinking it was black and white overkill.  With 24 hours going out and picking up something with more colour wasn''t a real option. To grow its vote?: As above - the cropping - or framing of the picture I think counted to the negative - but shot the person who gave you a one ... I can''t see how! Summary: Love it .. great shot .. .keep shooting. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari', '2006-03-24 01:33:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253591, 305325, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.<br/> <br/>Great fun to do a critique on your image but it is difficult if you don''t give us any information in your photographers comments.  When we do a critique, we go past just the photographic result, that''s what voters comments do.  The critique looks at what you were trying to achieve, how you wanted it to look and what issues you had in getting the image captured and ready for voting.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>Gorgeous - young and impressionable - well that is t he image anyway.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>I really do like this .. the rule of thirds appears to have bene applied 9 knowingly or not) ... <br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>I don''t know that this meets the challenge - those do not look like bi-focals (which often have a line across them where the split in the lens is) ... others may have picked this up .. as noted by kiwinick ...  I think that the earring may be overkill - only as it is quite a distinicly different colour of pink.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>You have some interesting comments on this in the comments recieved .. I think that she is quite well lit and I like the colours in the picture - the use of the pink keeps it in the realness of her young age ... other colours I think may be too harsh.<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>most of the things that i can think of would take time, you did not have the luxery ...  I think you did an exceptional job and well deserved a place in the top 20!<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>Well done .. lovely shot .. more details help me when commenting.<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. Great fun to do a critique on your image but it is difficult if you don''t give us any information in your photographers comments.  When we do a critique, we go past just the photographic result, that''s what voters comments do.  The critique looks at what you were trying to achieve, how you wanted it to look and what issues you had in getting the image captured and ready for voting. First Impression - the most important one: Gorgeous - young and impressionable - well that is t he image anyway. Composition: I really do like this .. the rule of thirds appears to have bene applied 9 knowingly or not) ...  Subject: I don''t know that this meets the challenge - those do not look like bi-focals (which often have a line across them where the split in the lens is) ... others may have picked this up .. as noted by kiwinick ...  I think that the earring may be overkill - only as it is quite a distinicly different colour of pink. Technical (Colour and light): You have some interesting comments on this in the comments recieved .. I think that she is quite well lit and I like the colours in the picture - the use of the pink keeps it in the realness of her young age ... other colours I think may be too harsh. To grow its vote?: most of the things that i can think of would take time, you did not have the luxery ...  I think you did an exceptional job and well deserved a place in the top 20! Summary: Well done .. lovely shot .. more details help me when commenting. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari', '2006-03-24 01:44:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253638, 305436, 43434, '<td valign="top">Critique as above .. posted in wrong window :)<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-03-24 01:55:32.</i></td>', 'Critique as above .. posted in wrong window :)', '2006-03-24 01:53:20.000000', '2006-03-24 01:55:32.000000', 0),
(2253643, 305436, 43434, '<td valign="top">::: Critique Club ::: <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. <br/> <br/>Interesting to do a critique on your image but it is difficult if you don''t give us any information in your photographers comments. When we do a critique, we go past just the photographic result, that''s what voters comments do. The critique looks at what you were trying to achieve, how you wanted it to look and what issues you had in getting the image captured and ready for voting. <br/> <br/>First Impression - the most important one: <br/>I really thought this was great .. a nice play on words .. and a good complementary colours. <br/> <br/>Composition: <br/>I think that you have cropped this well ... It is a little too centred for my true liking ... but I think this is learned through learning the rules of photography like the rule of thirds. <br/> <br/>Subject: <br/>meets the challenge and does it nicely. <br/> <br/>Technical (Colour and light): <br/>some have said the colours are flat but I think they are not bad ... it is a good Depth of Field ... and I like the focus for this picture. <br/> <br/>To grow its vote?: <br/>Keep thinking about your shots and how you crop things .. sometimes by having a slightly less zommed in shot and creating a wider berth around the image creates a lovely feeling of it being larger than it is ... <br/> <br/>Summary: <br/>I think this is a good solid shot... for 24 hours you did really good. <br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. <br/> <br/>Cheers <br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.  Interesting to do a critique on your image but it is difficult if you don''t give us any information in your photographers comments. When we do a critique, we go past just the photographic result, that''s what voters comments do. The critique looks at what you were trying to achieve, how you wanted it to look and what issues you had in getting the image captured and ready for voting.  First Impression - the most important one: I really thought this was great .. a nice play on words .. and a good complementary colours.  Composition: I think that you have cropped this well ... It is a little too centred for my true liking ... but I think this is learned through learning the rules of photography like the rule of thirds.  Subject: meets the challenge and does it nicely.  Technical (Colour and light): some have said the colours are flat but I think they are not bad ... it is a good Depth of Field ... and I like the focus for this picture.  To grow its vote?: Keep thinking about your shots and how you crop things .. sometimes by having a slightly less zommed in shot and creating a wider berth around the image creates a lovely feeling of it being larger than it is ...  Summary: I think this is a good solid shot... for 24 hours you did really good.  If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.  Cheers  Kari', '2006-03-24 01:54:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253671, 305159, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.<br/> <br/>Interesting to do a critique on your image but it is difficult if you don''t give us any information in your photographers comments.  When we do a critique, we go past just the photographic result, that''s what voters comments do.  The critique looks at what you were trying to achieve, how you wanted it to look and what issues you had in getting the image captured and ready for voting.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>For 24 hours not bad ... but with minor improvements could do way better.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>Fotoman expressed it best .. change the shot to portrait and I would see a great improvement .. I found the background distracting - but it works ... also cropping the picture of what is not really needed helps a shot greatly.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>Meets the challenge although I am not sure that those are bi-focals.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>I like the lighting and think this has been done well ... the colours are a little wishy washing to me.<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>Take note of what your commentors said .. and apply things that you learn on the way.<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>Kep trying .. you are doing good - keep it up.<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. Interesting to do a critique on your image but it is difficult if you don''t give us any information in your photographers comments.  When we do a critique, we go past just the photographic result, that''s what voters comments do.  The critique looks at what you were trying to achieve, how you wanted it to look and what issues you had in getting the image captured and ready for voting. First Impression - the most important one: For 24 hours not bad ... but with minor improvements could do way better. Composition: Fotoman expressed it best .. change the shot to portrait and I would see a great improvement .. I found the background distracting - but it works ... also cropping the picture of what is not really needed helps a shot greatly. Subject: Meets the challenge although I am not sure that those are bi-focals. Technical (Colour and light): I like the lighting and think this has been done well ... the colours are a little wishy washing to me. To grow its vote?: Take note of what your commentors said .. and apply things that you learn on the way. Summary: Kep trying .. you are doing good - keep it up. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari', '2006-03-24 02:01:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253705, 305282, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.<br/> <br/>Just a note when I do a critique, I go past just the photographic result, that''s what voters comments do.  The critique looks at what you were trying to achieve, how you wanted it to look and what issues you had in getting the image captured and ready for voting - somtimes it is nice for these details also to be included in the photographers comments.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>Using what you got did you good!<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>I really like this composition - It has really strong lines .. and good colours in it.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>Only partially meets the challenge - bi-focals I am not sure these are ... this has been reised by others who commented and may have affected your score - although not greatly I think .. but just a small details helps to improve things.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>I really like the lighting and the colour is fantastic ... this is a sexy shot!<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>Using the right stuff (if these are I apologise) - a little rotation may have have helped ... I think to the left .. to make the shot standing not look like it is on an angle.<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>Great shot ... good shooting.<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari<br/>======</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. Just a note when I do a critique, I go past just the photographic result, that''s what voters comments do.  The critique looks at what you were trying to achieve, how you wanted it to look and what issues you had in getting the image captured and ready for voting - somtimes it is nice for these details also to be included in the photographers comments. First Impression - the most important one: Using what you got did you good! Composition: I really like this composition - It has really strong lines .. and good colours in it. Subject: Only partially meets the challenge - bi-focals I am not sure these are ... this has been reised by others who commented and may have affected your score - although not greatly I think .. but just a small details helps to improve things. Technical (Colour and light): I really like the lighting and the colour is fantastic ... this is a sexy shot! To grow its vote?: Using the right stuff (if these are I apologise) - a little rotation may have have helped ... I think to the left .. to make the shot standing not look like it is on an angle. Summary: Great shot ... good shooting. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari======', '2006-03-24 02:07:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253740, 305405, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.<br/> <br/>How am I meant to do this .. you got the blue!<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>I really thought this was cool ... such a true kiwi number 8 wire job.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>I discussed quickly with the lover .. and he said it wasn''t sharp ... but I figure it is hard to make a chicken stay still!<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>Meets the challenge with great gusto.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>I love the colours and natural lighting.<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>Like you can!<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/> <br/>Bloody hell bandit you give us kiwis something to aim for ... :) well done.<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. How am I meant to do this .. you got the blue! First Impression - the most important one: I really thought this was cool ... such a true kiwi number 8 wire job. Composition: I discussed quickly with the lover .. and he said it wasn''t sharp ... but I figure it is hard to make a chicken stay still! Subject: Meets the challenge with great gusto. Technical (Colour and light): I love the colours and natural lighting. To grow its vote?: Like you can! Summary:  Bloody hell bandit you give us kiwis something to aim for ... :) well done. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari', '2006-03-24 02:15:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253788, 305138, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>Ok .. so you are sick of seeing ... "where did you get all those pick feathers" .. I am surprised you just didn''t admit killing a pink flamingo!<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/> <br/>not bad .. I would have like the shows to hit the thirds a little better .. but they are nicely hidden in the feathers.  <br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>Yup meets the challenge.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>Nice lighting ... interesting colour, interesing use of DOF ... some liked others didn''t .. . so that is reflected in the voting ... it is however a nice shot.<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>Think about the location of the shoes in the bluster of feathers ... trying a number of different crops till one falls right may also help.  <br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>Your highest score so far - and you only had hours - you are improving .. can''t wait to see more of what you manage in a week.<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. First Impression - the most important one: Ok .. so you are sick of seeing ... "where did you get all those pick feathers" .. I am surprised you just didn''t admit killing a pink flamingo! Composition:  not bad .. I would have like the shows to hit the thirds a little better .. but they are nicely hidden in the feathers.   Subject: Yup meets the challenge. Technical (Colour and light): Nice lighting ... interesting colour, interesing use of DOF ... some liked others didn''t .. . so that is reflected in the voting ... it is however a nice shot. To grow its vote?: Think about the location of the shoes in the bluster of feathers ... trying a number of different crops till one falls right may also help.   Summary: Your highest score so far - and you only had hours - you are improving .. can''t wait to see more of what you manage in a week. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari', '2006-03-24 02:30:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253819, 305371, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/> <br/>Love the de-sat (can''t often say that) ... and the smile on her face ... she may have been nervous but she did well.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>I liked this ... you have the bottom rails on thirds which is nice ... having it with her a little further into the shot may have created a leaving something as well as a going somewhere impression.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>Crikey this meets the challenge and does it good.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>I really think that this look works well for this picture.  <br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>as mentioned above ... that is all i can think of.<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>I think thsi is really lovely .. and I hope you looked after your model after she fell off.<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. First Impression - the most important one:  Love the de-sat (can''t often say that) ... and the smile on her face ... she may have been nervous but she did well. Composition: I liked this ... you have the bottom rails on thirds which is nice ... having it with her a little further into the shot may have created a leaving something as well as a going somewhere impression. Subject: Crikey this meets the challenge and does it good. Technical (Colour and light): I really think that this look works well for this picture.   To grow its vote?: as mentioned above ... that is all i can think of. Summary: I think thsi is really lovely .. and I hope you looked after your model after she fell off. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari', '2006-03-24 02:41:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(2253861, 305305, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>What a frame!<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/> <br/>This is nice .. centred as it needs to be ... but the impression of a love heart adds to the composition.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>meets the challenge - in face you nailed it.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>Great ... I like the relfections of light on the shoes as well .. <br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>I find it hard on a top five to think how to improve ... take note of the other commemntors though :)<br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>Gorgeous ... really good solid shot.<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. First Impression - the most important one: What a frame! Composition:  This is nice .. centred as it needs to be ... but the impression of a love heart adds to the composition. Subject: meets the challenge - in face you nailed it. Technical (Colour and light): Great ... I like the relfections of light on the shoes as well ..  To grow its vote?: I find it hard on a top five to think how to improve ... take note of the other commemntors though :) Summary: Gorgeous ... really good solid shot. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari', '2006-03-24 02:48:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(2254038, 305216, 19831, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :-)<br/> <br/>This was your entry for the ''Pick Two'' challenge. Definitely meets the challenge, there are indeed feathers and high heeled shoes :-) Congrats on the top 20 for this lovely photo. This is a tough one to critique, I frankly see nothing ''wrong'' with the photo at all. I could maybe suggest things to do different, but they would only be that, different, and not at all necessarily ''improvement'' :-) But I shall try and give you some of my thoughts. <br/> <br/>I really like the low contrast approach here. The image is very pleasing to look at, and has a very sensuous feel to it. It lends itself well to the processing you have done, IMO. The positioning of the legs is great. That and the slight arch in the back I feel contribute a lot to the ''feel'' of the photo. It might be nice to see a little bit more of the feet, although I wouldn''t change the positioning of the legs/feet at all, and I don''t know if changing the position of the shoes and boa might alter the composition adversely or not. The dark inner side of the heel of the shoe kind of keeps pulling my eye to it. One of your commenters mentioned perhaps lying one shoe on the side, maybe this could prevent that darkness from being as prominent? One of your comments mentions the slight lack of detail in feathers and shoes, but I''d guess that in the full size/res photo, it probably looks great. <br/> <br/>This is just a really fine photo. The low contrast treatment quickly grabs your attention, and then the soft sensuous feel of the image demands the viewer lingers a while to fully enjoy the presentation. Very nicely done, congrats :-) <br/> <br/>If you have any questions or comments or anything, please feel free to contact me. <br/> <br/>Happy Shooting, <br/>taterbug :-)</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :-) This was your entry for the ''Pick Two'' challenge. Definitely meets the challenge, there are indeed feathers and high heeled shoes :-) Congrats on the top 20 for this lovely photo. This is a tough one to critique, I frankly see nothing ''wrong'' with the photo at all. I could maybe suggest things to do different, but they would only be that, different, and not at all necessarily ''improvement'' :-) But I shall try and give you some of my thoughts.  I really like the low contrast approach here. The image is very pleasing to look at, and has a very sensuous feel to it. It lends itself well to the processing you have done, IMO. The positioning of the legs is great. That and the slight arch in the back I feel contribute a lot to the ''feel'' of the photo. It might be nice to see a little bit more of the feet, although I wouldn''t change the positioning of the legs/feet at all, and I don''t know if changing the position of the shoes and boa might alter the composition adversely or not. The dark inner side of the heel of the shoe kind of keeps pulling my eye to it. One of your commenters mentioned perhaps lying one shoe on the side, maybe this could prevent that darkness from being as prominent? One of your comments mentions the slight lack of detail in feathers and shoes, but I''d guess that in the full size/res photo, it probably looks great.  This is just a really fine photo. The low contrast treatment quickly grabs your attention, and then the soft sensuous feel of the image demands the viewer lingers a while to fully enjoy the presentation. Very nicely done, congrats :-)  If you have any questions or comments or anything, please feel free to contact me.  Happy Shooting, taterbug :-)', '2006-03-24 04:10:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(2254870, 305182, 43148, '<td valign="top">Well, sorry all, but flash was NOT used, was trying for a center of focus with a hand held table light. That is why it gets so dark so quickly behind pb jar, light did not extend there, course it did make the black counter look a bit grey, but that type of thing happens often with strong, direct light. Sorry again, LOL, but I still think it is an okay photo for what I was trying to  do, wish more of you had thought the same!! <br/> <br/>Jacque</td>', 'Well, sorry all, but flash was NOT used, was trying for a center of focus with a hand held table light. That is why it gets so dark so quickly behind pb jar, light did not extend there, course it did make the black counter look a bit grey, but that type of thing happens often with strong, direct light. Sorry again, LOL, but I still think it is an okay photo for what I was trying to  do, wish more of you had thought the same!!  Jacque', '2006-03-24 12:38:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(2256418, 305360, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Very interesting idea for the Pick Two challenge, you have defiantly meet the requirements of the challenge itself.  I am surprised that you scored below a 5, but I guess that just how the voting goes someways.  <br/> <br/>The image itself has a nice soft feel, very artistic. The covers are nice and soft while still leaving enough glow on the peanut butter to bring out the subject.  The overall composition is nice, I wish the stuff in the upper left and right could have been cropped out, it would probably have been pretty hard with leaving the dogs ears intact.  This is really a great concept, I am surprised it didn''t go over better.  Maybe the soft glow feels a little too processed for some.  The lighting looks like the sun is going down but is very appropriate for this piece.  Good work on capturing the shot, I know my dog would not have been able to sit still with the peanut butter there.  I think the placement of the dog in the background is just prefect, it give the shot a nice balance.</td>', '*Critique Club* Very interesting idea for the Pick Two challenge, you have defiantly meet the requirements of the challenge itself.  I am surprised that you scored below a 5, but I guess that just how the voting goes someways.   The image itself has a nice soft feel, very artistic. The covers are nice and soft while still leaving enough glow on the peanut butter to bring out the subject.  The overall composition is nice, I wish the stuff in the upper left and right could have been cropped out, it would probably have been pretty hard with leaving the dogs ears intact.  This is really a great concept, I am surprised it didn''t go over better.  Maybe the soft glow feels a little too processed for some.  The lighting looks like the sun is going down but is very appropriate for this piece.  Good work on capturing the shot, I know my dog would not have been able to sit still with the peanut butter there.  I think the placement of the dog in the background is just prefect, it give the shot a nice balance.', '2006-03-24 20:43:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(2256441, 305083, 41802, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>"Off to work." is a wonderful entry into the Pick Two Challenge.  I think it is so great when you exceeds your expectations of the shot you have entered.  <br/> <br/>The image is obviously very well put together.  Black and white was an excellent choice, to enhance all of those key features, like the rings on the shoes and accent letters on the bike.  The composition is excellent, the shot feel complete.  I think the black border is a nice addition.  I really can see anything I would change, you just did an excellent job.  <br/> <br/>Congrats on the Top Ten finish, especially since this is your first official member challenge! Your wife is an awesome support my husband has to pretty much be dragged kicking and screaming into any shot.</td>', '*Critique Club* "Off to work." is a wonderful entry into the Pick Two Challenge.  I think it is so great when you exceeds your expectations of the shot you have entered.   The image is obviously very well put together.  Black and white was an excellent choice, to enhance all of those key features, like the rings on the shoes and accent letters on the bike.  The composition is excellent, the shot feel complete.  I think the black border is a nice addition.  I really can see anything I would change, you just did an excellent job.   Congrats on the Top Ten finish, especially since this is your first official member challenge! Your wife is an awesome support my husband has to pretty much be dragged kicking and screaming into any shot.', '2006-03-24 20:57:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(2256710, 305108, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Yep, good one.  Congratulations on your fine finish with this image for the Pick Two Challenge.<br/> <br/>Composition is good, meets the Challenge head on and has the simplicity that is so pleasing to DPC voters.<br/> <br/>If it was mine, I''d probably have tried for a bit more clarity in the feather and in the spokes.  But this image is yours, not mine, and you did well with it.<br/> <br/>Keep up the good work.<br/> <br/>Alice</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Yep, good one.  Congratulations on your fine finish with this image for the Pick Two Challenge. Composition is good, meets the Challenge head on and has the simplicity that is so pleasing to DPC voters. If it was mine, I''d probably have tried for a bit more clarity in the feather and in the spokes.  But this image is yours, not mine, and you did well with it. Keep up the good work. Alice', '2006-03-24 23:17:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(2256993, 305398, 43434, '<td valign="top"><b><i>::: Critique Club :::</i></b> <br/> <br/>Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club.<br/> <br/><b>First Impression - the most important one:</b> <br/>Interesting incorporation of the feather with the heels ... well done.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>This is not bad.  The problem areas have already  been raised and this is the grate behind the subject and the flooring.  Both of which are distracting elements.<br/> <br/><b>Subject:</b> <br/>Meets the challenge.<br/> <br/><b>Technical (Colour and light):</b> <br/>Goof lighting ... reasonable colour and good focus.  With a change of background you could have played more with colour I think.<br/> <br/><b>To grow its vote?:</b> <br/>Think about where you are shooting.  I have find it extremely important to not have a busy background or base (floor) for the picture unless it is intentional. <br/> <br/><b>Summary:</b> <br/>Good pic .. well done for this challenge.  And so far away from what you have been shooting .... never be afraid of trying new things.<br/> <br/>If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system.<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/> <br/>Kari</td>', '::: Critique Club :::  Hi, my name is Kari and from the critique club. First Impression - the most important one: Interesting incorporation of the feather with the heels ... well done. Composition: This is not bad.  The problem areas have already  been raised and this is the grate behind the subject and the flooring.  Both of which are distracting elements. Subject: Meets the challenge. Technical (Colour and light): Goof lighting ... reasonable colour and good focus.  With a change of background you could have played more with colour I think. To grow its vote?: Think about where you are shooting.  I have find it extremely important to not have a busy background or base (floor) for the picture unless it is intentional.  Summary: Good pic .. well done for this challenge.  And so far away from what you have been shooting .... never be afraid of trying new things. If you''ve got any questions about this critique, please feel free to contact me via the PM system. Cheers Kari', '2006-03-25 01:09:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(2257165, 305339, 32997, '<td valign="top">beautiful portrait...well done..</td>', 'beautiful portrait...well done..', '2006-03-25 02:54:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(2257805, 305168, 9906, '<td valign="top">From the Critique Club:<br/> <br/>This is IMHO a perfect example of a technically well done shot that didn''t score very well in the challenge.  You have good DOF and focus.  Your lighting is very nice.  You have a nice white background but a little bit of grey around the base of the peanut butter cup.  Your exposure is a touch off.  The frosting on the peanut butter is a little blown out losing some of the detail.  Well thought out and executed.<br/> <br/>Why didn''t it do so well?  I think it is because the overall impact of the shot is a touch unappealing.  I don''t understand the reason for putting the jello inside or on top of the jar.  Green ''peanut butter'' makes me go eeewwww.  So though this shot is well executed, it''s not the kind of shot that scores well in this medium called DPC.<br/> <br/>Yours<br/>TC</td>', 'From the Critique Club: This is IMHO a perfect example of a technically well done shot that didn''t score very well in the challenge.  You have good DOF and focus.  Your lighting is very nice.  You have a nice white background but a little bit of grey around the base of the peanut butter cup.  Your exposure is a touch off.  The frosting on the peanut butter is a little blown out losing some of the detail.  Well thought out and executed. Why didn''t it do so well?  I think it is because the overall impact of the shot is a touch unappealing.  I don''t understand the reason for putting the jello inside or on top of the jar.  Green ''peanut butter'' makes me go eeewwww.  So though this shot is well executed, it''s not the kind of shot that scores well in this medium called DPC. YoursTC', '2006-03-25 12:33:07.000000', NULL, 0),
